serious allegations of past safety  practices and the workplace culture at  blue origin that's the space company  founded by billionaire jeff bezos blue  origin has had 17 consecutive successful  launches all of them cleared by the faa  however in her only tv interview former  blue origin employee alexandra abrams  tells lori siegel of 60 minutes plus she  believes the company sacrificed safety  because bezos wanted to win the space  race abrams is the co-author of a new  essay along with 20 other current and  former blue origin employees and this is  an interview you will only see on cbs  mornings  when i first joined blue origin i felt  like i was in star trek  and i think i came to tears once when i  was saying how grateful i was to be a  part of this journey  from 2017 until 2019 alexandra abrams  worked in communications for blue origin  a private space company owned and  largely funded by amazon owner and one  of the richest men in the world jeff  bezos the culture was  one of collaboration  innovation  excitement enthusiasm  nerdiness which i just loved  but that did not  stay the same for very long  blue origin's plan is wildly ambitious  to enable a future where millions of  people live and work in space to benefit  earth  but abrams who signed a non-disclosure  and non-disparagement agreement when she  left blue origin in 2019 says the  company's public message is at odds with  the reality she saw inside  i've gotten far enough away from it that  i'm  not afraid enough to let them silence me  anymore  in the last few years blue origin has  joined a modern-day space race alongside  bezos's fellow billionaires elon musk  and richard branson  blue origin reached a milestone this  summer when it launched its first manned  space flight with owner jeff bezos on  board abram says she saw women demeaned  at the company but her frustration  boiled over when senior leadership asked  her to help roll out stricter agreements  that she says took away employees rights  to resolve most disputes in court or to  speak freely about alleged harassment or  discrimination  i spend my days telling employees how  we're saving humanity in the planet  and on the other end i'm having the wool  pulled over my eyes about  taking rights away from our employees it  was not only  the hypocrisy that bothered me  it was the fact that i was propagating  the hypocrisy  i was actively participating abram says  she pushed back  she was fired in 2019. i was very  shocked like totally shocked  maybe i shouldn't have been  and i looked at  my new manager and said  you know i'm really curious like  what's the reason you're firing me  and he said  bob and i can't trust you anymore  she's referring to bob smith the ceo of  blue origin  since she left abram says she got  together with several current and former  blue origin employees often at her house  using a burner laptop so they couldn't  be tracked the plan  write an essay for the online platform  lioness.co criticizing blue origin for  its workplace culture and safety  practices  the people who signed on to this essay  are  absolutely incredible  brave  brilliant  human beings i look at this group  and i see  fighters for truth  fighters for safety  fighters for quality the essay accuses  blue origin of being rife with sexism it  says some women warned new female hires  to stay away from one executive who  appeared to be protected by his close  personal relationship with bezos it took  him physically groping a female  subordinate for him to finally be let go  women at blue origin were absolutely  treated differently than men  their ideas were not valued  as much they were not listened to  the essay says that in 2018 one team had  documented more than 1 000 problem  reports related to the engines that  power blue origins rockets which had  never been addressed 13 of the 21  signees are engineers  or technical they span all the major  programs of the company  and they also span  different levels we spoke privately to  five of abram's co-authors who are  staying anonymous because they fear  being blacklisted in a specialized  industry  several mentioned pressure to compete in  the private space race impacted safety  concerns it was great that blue origin  was smooth and steady  and slow  until jeff started becoming impatient  and elon and branson were getting ahead  and then we started to feel this  increasing pressure and impatience  that would definitely filter down from  leadership  do you believe that competing with the  other billionaires in the space race  took precedence over safety  yes  i do believe that cbs news has obtained  this 2020 internal memo written by a  blue origin engineer as he exited the  company it was addressed to company  leadership and reads our path is not a  sufficiently safe path would you trust a  blue origin vehicle going to space  unfortunately lori i would not  trust a blue origin vehicle going to  space  two former blue origin employees we  spoke with agreed with abrams and three  sources said staying on schedule often  trumped safety i mean it's an  extraordinary statement to be putting  out an essay with folks who are  engineers some who potentially could  still be at the company  saying  we don't believe in the safety of our  product  you cannot  create a culture of safety  and a culture of fear at the same time  they are incompatible  often times when i would try to  reconcile what i was hearing from the  engineers who are close to the vehicle  versus leadership about risk and safety  i would often go to leadership and say  okay how am i supposed to think about  this and often the response would be  oh well that person in particular just  doesn't have a high enough risk  tolerance that was said to you yes  blue origin's next space mission is  scheduled for week after next a launch  with four astronauts on board  abram says her essay was sent to the faa  before it was published online for folks  who are watching this who  see that and who say that the pushback  is blue origin said you were fired for  poor performance because leaders said  they couldn't trust you  they might say this is why you're out  there criticizing the company if that  was true then how would i have gotten 20  current and former employees to  contribute to this effort including very  senior people if i was known as a poor  performer  and someone who had bad decision-making  skills within blue origin would any of  these people trust me these are smart  people as the former head of internal  communications for blue origin what  message would you have for jeff bezos  today  i think i would say to jeff  that i really wished he was the person  we all thought he was  and that blue origin was the company we  all thought it was going to be  in a statement to cbs news blue origin  did not address safety allegations in  the essay but they said the company has  no tolerance for discrimination or  harassment of any kind and will promptly  investigate any new claims of misconduct  now blue origin told us abrams was fired  after repeated warnings for issues  regarding federal export control  regulations abrams told cbs news she  never received any warnings regarding  issues of that nature lori i think  what's striking about this is blue  origin is supposed to be blazing the  future for all of us and according to  these allegations they are stuck in the  past and running a risky culture but  abrams we have to underscore was fired  yep why do we trust her yeah and it  hasn't been with the company for two  years yeah two years right you know i i  think this is a woman who has a lot to  lose she's going up against a very  powerful company a very powerful man um  where she could face legal recourse um  and you know i think she was in charge  of employee communications  at the company and i think she believes  this is her way of being able to have a  voice for people who don't believe that  they can speak out now  you know why should we trust her i also  spoke to multiple other sources who had  similar stories all these different  sources shared stories that all sounded  like one another and i heard the same  themes over and over again i i think  about those four astronauts they're  going to be on a blue origin flight i  think a week from now  it makes me wonder what you heard from  people whose names we don't know about  potential technical problems with blue  origin you know look i think the one  thing i really took away was  besides a toxic or sexist work  environment and folks speaking out  against that was this drive to stay on  schedule impacted safety and this fear  that people had  that folks like engineers didn't feel  like they could go to the leaders and  say something because it might push back  um you know i think i heard that over  and over again and so breeding that  culture isn't a good thing when you have  these rocket ships going to space and  you have human lives but they also had  17 consecutive successful launches all  cleared by the faa and i'm very curious  about the safety question considering  jeff bezos himself was on the first  launch yeah it seems like if if there  was such a thing as being concerned  about he wouldn't put himself exactly he  wouldn't put himself in harm's way yeah  i think it's a good question right and i  think it's an important thing to note  there have been 17 successful launches  right this is a company um that is  buzzed about and for good reason um i  also think it's important um you know  the overwhelming message i got from sp  from folks where they were afraid to  speak out and they were they feared  retaliation for speaking out and in the  future this could have an impact is what  they said and that's why they wanted to  take the risk and do this all right lori  siegel thank you
 

